 ## Overview
-SDF Domain Manipulation Operators modify the input position vector $\mathbf{p}$ to partition space into cells, creating periodic, mirrored, or reflected spatial structures for Signed Distance Functions (SDFs) [S1, S2, unofficial]. They transform the coordinate space before the primitive SDF is evaluated, allowing for the creation of complex patterns like grids, tiles, and symmetries with minimal computational overhead [S1, S2, unofficial]. Unlike [CSG Operations on Implicit Functions](1329-csg-operations-on-implicit-functions.md), which combines scalar fields via boolean logic, domain operators transform the geometry's location in space directly [S2, unofficial]. They differ from [Finite Repetition](2869-finite-repetition.md) by typically operating on infinite domains or specific symmetries rather than bounded grids, though they share the mechanism of cell-based coordinate mapping [S2, unofficial].
